<Project>
  <!--
    The configuration-name mapping (Debug-Linux, Release-Windows, Debug-VM, ...)
    lives in its own file because Directory.Build.targets imports it too, for the
    submodules whose own Directory.Build.props deliberately does not chain here.
    That file explains the asymmetry and why it matters.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)eng\Broiler.Configurations.props" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <EnableWindowsTargeting>true</EnableWindowsTargeting>

    <!--
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      Suppressed Build Warnings — Documentation
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

      The warnings below are suppressed because fixing them requires a
      refactor that is too large or risky for the current project scope.
      Each entry lists the warning code, description, reason for
      suppression, and suggested future action.

      ── Nullable Reference Type Warnings ──────────────────────────────
      CS8600 : Converting null literal or possible null value to non-nullable type
      CS8601 : Possible null reference assignment
      CS8602 : Dereference of a possibly null reference
      CS8603 : Possible null reference return
      CS8604 : Possible null reference argument
      CS8618 : Non-nullable property/field must contain a non-null value when exiting constructor
      CS8619 : Nullability of reference types in value doesn't match target type
      CS8620 : Argument cannot be used for parameter due to differences in nullability
      CS8622 : Nullability of reference types in type of parameter doesn't match target delegate
      CS8625 : Cannot convert null literal to non-nullable reference type
      CS8631 : The type cannot be used as type parameter due to differences in nullability
      CS8632 : The annotation for nullable reference types should only be used in '#nullable' context
      CS8765 : Nullability of type of parameter doesn't match overridden member
      CS8767 : Nullability of reference types in type of parameter doesn't match implicitly implemented member

      Reason  : 1,466 warnings across 20+ projects. Fixing every instance
                would touch hundreds of files and risk breaking existing
                behavior.  The codebase has a mix of projects with and
                without <Nullable>enable</Nullable>, and many APIs pass
                null by design.
      Future  : Incrementally enable nullable and resolve warnings on a
                per-project basis when each project is actively maintained.

      ── Source-Generator Warnings ─────────────────────────────────────
      CS0109 : The member does not hide an accessible member. The 'new' keyword is not required.
      CS0628 : New protected member declared in sealed type.

      Reason  : Emitted by Broiler.JavaScript.JSClassGenerator in
                generated partial classes (e.g. JSNumber.g.cs,
                JSBigInt.g.cs).  The generator uses 'new' unconditionally
                and creates protected constructors regardless of whether
                the target class is sealed.
      Future  : Update the source generator to conditionally emit 'new'
                and adjust access modifiers based on the target class.

      RS1038 : Compiler extension in assembly with CodeAnalysis.Workspaces reference.
      RS1041 : Compiler extension targeting .NET 8.0 instead of netstandard2.0.

      Reason  : The JSClassGenerator source generator project references
                Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Workspaces and targets
                net8.0.  Changing the target framework and removing the
                Workspaces dependency requires a generator rewrite.
      Future  : Retarget the generator to netstandard2.0 and replace
                Workspaces APIs with Roslyn source-generation APIs.

      ── Unread Primary-Constructor Parameters ─────────────────────────
      CS9113 : Parameter is unread.

      Reason  : Several primary-constructor parameters exist for API
                surface / structural reasons (e.g. JavaScriptObject(in
                Arguments a), ModuleCache(bool v), ILTryBlock label
                parameter) and are intentionally unused in the class body.
                Removing them would change public API signatures.
      Future  : Evaluate each parameter individually; remove or use those
                that are truly unnecessary.

      ── Platform Compatibility ────────────────────────────────────────
      CA1416 : Call site is reachable on all platforms but API is
               Windows-only.

      Reason  : The HTML rendering adapters (WpfAdapter, SkiaImageAdapter,
                PenAdapter) use System.Drawing and WPF types that are
                inherently Windows-only.  The projects deliberately target
                net8.0 (not net8.0-windows) to keep the shared-library
                surface cross-platform where possible.
      Future  : Add [SupportedOSPlatform("windows")] attributes to the
                adapter classes, or split Windows-specific code into
                platform-targeted projects.

      ── Module Initializers ───────────────────────────────────────────
      CA2255 : The 'ModuleInitializer' attribute is only intended for
               application code or advanced source generator scenarios.

      Reason  : ModuleInitializer is used intentionally in seven
                JavaScript engine assemblies to auto-register built-in
                types at assembly load time.  This is the intended
                advanced scenario referenced by the rule.
      Future  : No action needed; this is a valid use case.

      ── Obsolete API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
      SYSLIB0013 : Uri.EscapeUriString is obsolete.

      Reason  : Used to implement JavaScript's encodeURI() function.
                Uri.EscapeUriString matches encodeURI() semantics more
                closely than Uri.EscapeDataString (which over-encodes
                reserved URI characters).  Replacing it risks breaking
                JS spec compliance.
      Future  : Implement a custom encoder that matches the ECMAScript
                encodeURI() specification exactly.
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS8600;CS8601;CS8602;CS8603;CS8604;CS8618;CS8619;CS8620;CS8622;CS8625;CS8631;CS8632;CS8765;CS8767;CS0109;CS0628;CS9113;CA1416;CA2255;SYSLIB0013;RS1038;RS1041</NoWarn>

    <!--
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      PROMOTED TO AN ERROR. The block above is what this repository has
      decided to tolerate; these are the two things it has decided not to.

      Both are one defect seen from two sides. Retiring
      Runtime/JsInterop.cs means retyping the bridge's engine-typed
      storage — the wrapper roots (DomBridge.cs:98-100) and the listener
      record (DomBridge/RuntimeStates.cs:7) — from JSObject and JSValue,
      which are CLASSES, to JsValue, which is a STRUCT. What each reader
      of those fields does on the day that happens, MEASURED in a probe
      project rather than assumed:

          f is null              error CS0037    loud
          f is not { }           error CS8518    loud
          f == null              warning CS8073  ALWAYS FALSE
          f != null              warning CS8073  ALWAYS TRUE
          ReferenceEquals(f, g)  warning CA2013  boxes; false forever
          f is { } x             nothing at all  always true, silently

      The bottom three are why this block exists, and being warnings is
      no safety net: NOTHING IN THIS REPOSITORY READS WARNINGS.
      .github/workflows/ci.yml runs `dotnet build` with no warnaserror
      switch and no TreatWarningsAsErrors, so a warning is a line of
      green log. Every id promoted here is one the compiler already
      knows and would otherwise mention to nobody.

      (This enumeration used to name "the registry" as a third store
      still to be retyped. JsObjectRegistry was re-typed onto JsValue in
      45607c6 and holds Dictionary<DomNode, JsValue>; the identical stale
      clause was removed from Runtime/JsInterop.cs and from
      eng/jseal-budget.json earlier in this series. It had reached three
      files.)
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

      ── Reference equality on a value type ────────────────────────────
      CA2013 : Do not pass an argument with value type to
               ReferenceEquals.

      Reason  : It compiles, it boxes both sides, and it answers false
                forever — measurably so: ReferenceEquals(v, v) on one
                struct VALUE is False, not merely on two copies of it.

                One call site says what that costs.
                DomBridge/EventTargetInterface.cs:138 reaches
                `_windowJSObject is { } window && ReferenceEquals(
                receiver, window)`. This rule catches the ReferenceEquals
                half; the `is { }` half stays silent (see below).
                Uncaught, every window-level listener would silently stop
                registering.

                There are 84 call sites under src/ for it to happen at.
                The filter that matters is `*.cs`: an unfiltered
                `grep -r src` also reads build output and finds four more
                inside .dll binaries, which is where 88 comes from.
                Restricting to TRACKED .cs files gives the same 84 and,
                unlike an exclude-the-build-directories spelling, does not
                depend on whether a build has run:

                  git ls-files src | grep '\.cs$' |
                    xargs grep -hoE "ReferenceEquals[[:space:]]*\(" | wc -l

                (spelled without long options because an XML comment may
                not contain a double hyphen.)

                THIS LINE USED TO SAY 87, AND 87 WAS NEVER A COUNT OF CALL
                SITES. It is what `git grep -n ReferenceEquals` answered
                at 47b0f15, the commit that wrote it: a count of LINES
                MENTIONING the token, three of them comment prose. The
                call-site count that day was 85. The command is written
                down so the next person re-measures the same thing.
      Future  : It stays. When JsInterop is gone the rule guards the same
                mistake for whatever holds a handle next; a value-typed
                handle is the design, not a phase.

      ── A handle compared against null ────────────────────────────────
      CS8073 : The result of the expression is always 'false' / 'true',
               since a value of type 'T' is never equal to 'null' of
               type 'T?'.

      Reason  : The other half of the same retype, and the worse half,
                because BOTH SPELLINGS FAIL OPEN. `f == null` is a
                bail-out guard that stops bailing out; `f != null` is a
                proceed-only-if-present guard that always proceeds. An
                absent handle is JsValue.Missing, never null, so a test
                that used to mean "absent" comes to mean nothing and the
                code runs on with an empty handle.

                Three sites are written this way today, and all three are
                CORRECT today, because the field is still a nullable
                class:

                  DomBridge.WindowLoad.cs:346
                    `_realm is not { } realm || _windowJSObject == null`
                    The guard disappears; window events would dispatch
                    against an absent window.
                  DomBridge/LayoutMetrics.Scrolling.cs:448
                    `viewport == null || …Count == 0`
                    The listener-count clause still guards, so the
                    listeners run — and see an empty handle as the
                    event's target.
                  Features/EventListenerBinding.cs:120
                    `value != null && !value.IsNullOrUndefined && …`
                    Becomes CS8073 only once the listener record is
                    retyped; the clause after it still guards, so this
                    one degrades rather than breaks.

                Nothing trips this rule today, checked by a NON
                INCREMENTAL build, which matters: an incremental
                `dotnet build` of these solutions skips every CoreCompile
                and reports zero warnings without compiling a line. With
                the promotion in place, all four solutions rebuild with
                0 errors (Windows and Tests at Release and Release-VM,
                Linux and Android at Release), and no log holds a single
                `warning CS8073`, `error CS8073` or CS0472 of either kind.

                (A rebuild of either solution does report 80 warnings, and
                that is worth knowing because incremental runs have made
                "0 warnings" folklore here. Every one of the 80 comes from
                a submodule (Broiler.JavaScript.*, Broiler.HTML.*,
                Broiler.CSS, Broiler.Layout) and nothing under src/
                warns at all.)

                Nor is the promotion novel. 38 of this graph's ~94
                compilations already pass a bare /warnaserror+, so CS8073
                has been an error in them all along; every project under
                src/ is in the half that does not. And it is live
                everywhere it now applies: CS8073 is reported only at
                WarningLevel 5 and above, and this graph compiles at
                /warn:10 apart from three projects at /warn:8.
      Future  : It stays, for the reason CA2013 stays.

      ── The shape NEITHER of them sees ────────────────────────────────
      `f is { } x` on a struct is legal, always true, and carries NO
      diagnostic — not a warning, not a message. There is nothing to
      promote. Ten sites in Broiler.HtmlBridge.Dom read an engine-typed
      field this way, and the retype changes what four of them answer:

        DomBridge/DomBridge.CanvasHost.cs:16          : JsValue.Undefined
        DomBridge/Registration/CustomElements.cs:240  : JsValue.Null
        DomBridge.MessagingHost.cs:30                 : JsValue.Null
        DomBridge.WindowContextHost.cs:29             : JsValue.Undefined

      Each answers a DIFFERENT value for "no window yet" than the Missing
      a retyped field carries, and Missing is not Undefined and is not
      Null — the bridge reads argument presence on that distinction.
      CanvasHost's own comment states the semantics that would go: "A
      bridge that has not registered a window yet answers `undefined`,
      which is what the module's `IsObject` guard reads." Drop the
      `is { }` mechanically at any of those four and the value changes
      with nothing to say so. The other six fall back to Missing and are
      safe to collapse.

      This paragraph replaces the claim that every other reader "fails
      loudly when that happens". Most do. These do not, and they are the
      ones to read before retyping a root.

      ── The other shape neither of them sees ──────────────────────────
      JsValue.ObjectIdentity is declared `object?`
      (src/Broiler.HtmlBridge.Jseal/Values/JsValue.cs:98), so

          ReferenceEquals(a.ObjectIdentity, b.ObjectIdentity)

      compares two REFERENCE types. CA2013 is blind to it by
      construction — measured: in one compilation the raw struct form
      produced two CA2013 errors while the ObjectIdentity form on the next
      line produced none — and it answers TRUE for any two non-object
      handles, because ObjectIdentity is null for every one of them: two
      different numbers, two Undefineds, two Missings. No site writes it
      today. Six sites pass ObjectIdentity onward as a
      ConditionalWeakTable key and every one of them null-guards first, so
      the collision is unreachable rather than absent.

      ── How far this reaches, and where it does not ───────────────────
      As far as the props chain. All 13 projects under src/, plus the
      submodules whose own Directory.Build.props calls GetPathOfFileAbove:
      Broiler.CSS (and its nested Broiler.DOM), Broiler.DOM,
      Broiler.Graphics, Broiler.HTML, Broiler.Layout and Broiler.UI.
      Broiler.JS and Broiler.VM are STANDALONE — their props import their
      own eng/ files and nothing above — so neither engine resolves either
      id, either way. Neither holds a JsValue, so there is nothing in them
      to guard. This corrects "the whole tree was built with it as an
      error", which was over-broad.

      Nor is this a rule only src/ can trip. Broiler.Graphics declares
      seven `readonly struct` types with an `operator ==` (BColor,
      BMatrix3x2, BPoint, BRect, BSize, BImageHandle, BResourceHandle),
      which is exactly the CS8073-capable shape, and Broiler.HTML and
      Broiler.UI each vendor a copy of them. All compile clean today.

      TWO PLACES THE FOUR ROOT SOLUTIONS DO NOT REACH, so that a future
      check knows where to look. Broiler.UI/Broiler.Documents chains up to
      this file through Broiler.UI and appears in NO root solution: 159
      projects behind its own Broiler.Documents.slnx. And one src/ project
      sits outside the two builds usually taken for "the repository": Broiler.App.Android holds a ReferenceEquals call site
      (AndroidBroilerView.cs:119) and appears only in
      Broiler.Android.Browser.slnx, which CI builds at Release alone.
      Both rules reach it; only that one job proves it.

      ── Not promoted, and why ─────────────────────────────────────────
      CS0472 is the same diagnostic for a BUILT-IN value type
      (`i == null` on an int). No handle is a built-in, so it guards
      nothing in this migration.
    -->
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CA2013;CS8073</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    HtmlBridge complexity-reduction Phase 1 (project-graph repair), item 4: canonical
    single-source paths for the Broiler.Dom and Broiler.Graphics kernels.

    Two submodules carry a nested checkout of these kernels for their own standalone builds
    (Broiler.CSS/Broiler.DOM and Broiler.HTML/Broiler.Graphics). Left unmanaged, in-tree
    solution graphs could build two nodes with the same assembly name for each kernel. Each
    submodule's Directory.Build.props defaults $(BroilerDomPath)/$(BroilerGraphicsPath) to
    its nested copy (used only when built standalone). In root builds those submodule props
    chain up to THIS file, so the unconditional values below win and every consumer resolves
    to the single ROOT checkout — one Broiler.Dom node and one Graphics node per build graph.

    scripts/check-component-graph.sh fails CI when a solution's project closure still builds
    two assemblies of the same name, which is what a reference that escaped these properties
    looks like from the outside. It does not compare submodule SHAs: a nested checkout is
    expected to trail the root's pin, because the collapse means its sources are compiled
    against the root's copy and never its own, and whether they still compile is something
    the build says out loud.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BroilerDomPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.DOM\Broiler.Dom\Broiler.Dom.csproj</BroilerDomPath>
    <BroilerGraphicsPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.Graphics\src\Broiler.Graphics\Broiler.Graphics.csproj</BroilerGraphicsPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The same collapse for the kernels the Broiler.Graphics and Broiler.UI submodules vendor for their own standalone builds.
    It defaults $(BroilerMediaRoot) and $(BroilerInputRoot) to its nested checkouts and
    imports this file first, so the values below win in a root build and every Media and
    Input reference resolves to the single root checkout.

    Without this a root build produced two Broiler.Media.Image assemblies from two checkouts.
    They disagreed about whether ImageCodec.DecodeAsync is abstract, and whichever copy won
    the copy into the output directory decided whether the managed codecs could load at all:

      System.TypeLoadException: Method 'DecodeAsync' in type
      'Broiler.Media.Image.Managed.PngImageCodec' ... does not have an implementation.
  -->
  <!--
    Broiler.Layout stopped being a directory here on 2026-09-09 and became a submodule,
    because Broiler.HTML depends on it structurally and could not build alone while the
    only way to reach it was as a sibling of that repository. Its own build now resolves
    Broiler.CSS, Broiler.DOM and Broiler.Graphics through roots, and Broiler.HTML resolves
    Broiler.Layout, Broiler.CSS and Broiler.DOM the same way.

    Which means those roots have to be set HERE, or an in-tree build sends each submodule
    to its OWN nested checkout and the graph grows a second Broiler.Dom, a second
    Broiler.CSS and a second Broiler.Layout - the duplicate-assembly failure the two
    property groups above already exist to prevent, arriving by a new route.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BroilerCssRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.CSS</BroilerCssRoot>
    <BroilerDomRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.DOM</BroilerDomRoot>
    <BroilerLayoutRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.Layout</BroilerLayoutRoot>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <BroilerMediaRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.Media</BroilerMediaRoot>
    <BroilerInputRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.Input</BroilerInputRoot>
    <BroilerGraphicsRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Broiler.Graphics</BroilerGraphicsRoot>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
